provider_responses.text:
The main issue is that a software product has developed an unwanted high-priority notification pathway for certain users.

A particular label, screen element, or interaction sequence has become strongly linked to an automatic reaction. The goal is not to delete the underlying data or investigate every historical reason the association formed. The goal is to reduce that pathway’s priority so it no longer captures attention, triggers an automatic response, or dominates the user experience.

The risk is overcorrecting: repeatedly testing whether the notification still appears, analyzing its meaning, discussing it constantly, or adding more and more interventions can keep the pathway active. A better approach would be a consistent, limited redesign process:

- remove unnecessary prompts and repeated exposure checks;
- introduce neutral alternative workflows;
- keep the response simple and non-escalating;
- avoid turning the unwanted pathway into a major product feature through excessive monitoring;
- test only after a stable period, under ordinary usage conditions.

Success would mean the feature still technically exists in the system, but it no longer has special salience or automatic control over the user’s behavior.

